The foci of the first ellipse are  $(\pm 4,0)$  and the foci of the second ellipse are  $(20,10),(20,12)$ . By the definition of an ellipse, we have:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{(x-4)^2+y^2}+\sqrt{(x+4)^2+y^2}&=2a 
\sqrt{(x-20)^2+(y-12)^2}+\sqrt{(x-20)^2+(y-10)^2}&=2b.
\end{align*}
So
\begin{align*}
a+b&=\frac{\left(\sqrt{(x+4)^2+y^2}+\sqrt{(x-20)^2+(y-10)^2}\right)+\left(\sqrt{(x-4)^2+y^2}+\sqrt{(x-20)^2+(y-12)^2}\right)}{2} 
&\geq\frac{\text{dist}((-4,0),(20,10)+\text{dist}((4,0),(20,12))}{2}=\frac{20+26}{2}=23.
\end{align*}
Equality can occur, just draw a picture or solve for the intersection of the lines if you aren't convinced yet.

Note that 
\begin{align*}
\binom{\tbinom{n}{2}}{2}&=\binom{\tfrac{n^2-n}{2}}{2}
&=\frac{(n^2-n)(n^2-n-2)}{8}
&=\frac{(n+1)n(n-1)(n-2)}{8}
&=3\binom{n+1}{4}
\end{align*}
so we get 
\begin{align*}
\binom{\tbinom{3}{2}}{2} + \binom{\tbinom{4}{2}}{2} + \dots + \binom{\tbinom{40}{2}}{2}&=3\left(\binom{4}{4}+\binom{5}{4}+\dots+\binom{41}{4}\right)
&=3\binom{42}{5}
&=42\cdot 41\cdot 39\cdot 38
&=(40^2-4)(40^2-1)
&=40^4-5(40^2)+4
&\equiv \boxed{004}\pmod{1000},
\end{align*}
and we are done.

Fun problem.

Let the sum be  $S$ . Write it as 
\begin{align*}
S = \sum_{k=3}^{40} \binom{\binom{k}{2}}{2} 
= \sum_{k=3}^{40} \binom{\frac{k(k-1)}{2}}{2} 
= \sum_{k=3}^{40} \frac{\frac{k(k-1)}{2} \cdot \frac{(k-2)(k+1)}{2}}{2} 
= \frac18 \sum_{k=3}^{40} (k-2)(k-1)(k)(k+1) 
= \frac18 \sum_{k=3}^{40} 24 \binom{k+1}{4} 
= \frac18 \cdot 24 \cdot \binom{42}{5} 
\equiv 004 \pmod{1000} 
\end{align*} 
where we used the Hockey Stick Identity in the second to last line.

