This problem isn't that *hard*, it's just ... counterintuitive? Every little step and claim here looks simple but each took me a nontrivial amount of time to figure out.

Let  $p \leq n \leq 2p-2$  be a positive integer. We will show by induction on  $n$  that there exists such a subset  $B$  with  $|B| = n$  for every positive integer  $n$ .**<span style="color:blue">Base Case:**</span> Let  $k$  be the largest nonnegative integer such that all elements of  $A$  are congruent to some constant  $a$  mod  $p^k$ . Then suppose that infinitely many elements of  $A$  are congruent to  $k_1p^k + a$  mod  $p^{k+1}$  and that there exists at least one element congruent to  $k_2p^k + a$  mod  $p^{k+1}$  with  $k_1 \neq k_2$  by maximality of  $k$ . Then for the set  $B$  consisting of  $p-1$  copies of  $k_1p^k + a\bmod p^{k+1}$  elements and one copy of a  $k_2 p^k + a \bmod p^{k+1}$  element, we have 
\begin{align*}
\frac 1p \sum_{a \in S} a &= \frac{(p-1)\left(k_1p^k+a\right)+k_2p^k+a}p \pmod {p^{k+1}} 
&\equiv k_1p^k + (k_2-k_1)p^{k-1} + a \pmod {p^k}.
\end{align*}
This is not  $a$  mod  $p^k$ , so it cannot fall in  $A$ .**<span style="color:blue">Inductive Step:**</span> First, we prove the following claim.**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:**</span> Suppose that there are two distinct residues  $a$  and  $b$  mod  $p$  that appear infinitely often in  $A$ ; then we are done immediately.

*Proof:* Consider picking  $p-1$  copies of  $a$  and  $p-1$  copies of  $b$  mod  $p$ ; I claim that no average of  $p$  of these can even be an integer. Indeed, suppose we pick  $k$  copies of  $a$  and  $p - k$  copies of  $b$  in our set. Then we have, for some  $r \in \mathbb N$ , the system of equations
\begin{align*}
ka + (p-k)b &= rp 
a+b &= p.
\end{align*}
Subtracting  $k$  times the second equation from the first yields  $p \mid (p-2k)b$ , so  $p \mid b$ ; then  $p \mid a$  too, which contradicts distinctness.  $\blacksquare$ So now define  $k$  similarly as before such that  $k+1$  is the smallest positive integer with two distinct residues  $a$  and  $b$  mod  $p^{k+1}$  appearing in  $A$ . By the claim, there only exists one residue  $a$  that appears infinitely often in  $A$ ; thus let  $N$  be the largest positive integer in  $A$  not congruent to  $a$  mod  $p$ . Consider the inductive hypothesis for the set  $A' = A_{> N}$  to construct a set of  $n$  positive integers  $B'$  greater than  $N$  in  $A$  satisfying the conditions of the problem; these integers will all be  $a$  mod  $p^{k+1}$ .

Now, the set  $B = B' \cup \{N\}$  satisfies the conditions, as any average of  $p$  elements in  $B$  containing  $N$  will not be an integer, and the rest is guaranteed by the inductive hypothesis. This completes the induction.**<span style="color:purple">Remark:**</span> This problem is weird for a lot of reasons: induction on  $n$  doesn't make sense at first glance because the claim almost solves the problem entirely. Most of the solution simply attempts to ``patch" the claim in a way that still doesn't quite make sense to me (and in fact the cardinality of  $B$  is never used in the solution outside of the claim). Odd.

