I think that the CRT part of this problem is under-appreciated because there is a relatively clean general solution that works with higher modulo (like mod  $10^5$  or  $10^6$ ) that nobody has found yet.
-----
Let  $a_n = P_n(1)$ . We have  $a_2 = 121$  and  $a_{n+1} = a_n^5 + 10a_n^3 + 5a_n$  for  $n\ge 2$ . We want to find  $a_{2022}$  modulo  $8$  and  $125$ . 

Mod  $8$  is easy: we see that  $a_2\equiv 1\pmod{8}$ ,  $a_3\equiv 1+10+5\equiv 0\pmod{8}$ , and  $a_n\equiv 0\pmod{8}$  for  $n\ge 3$ .
--
Now, we compute  $a_{2022}\pmod{125}$ . The key idea is to compute  $a_n$  modulo increasing powers of  $5$ , and using the residue of each power to compute the residue of the next power.

First, we calculate mod  $5$ . Note that  $a_2\equiv 1\pmod{5}$  and  $a_{n+1}\equiv a_n^5\pmod{5}$  for  $n\ge 2$ . This means  $a_n\equiv 1\pmod{5}$  for  $n\ge 2$ .

Now, let  $a_n = 5b_n + 1$  for  $n\ge 2$ . By the Binomial Theorem, 
\begin{align*}
a_{n+1} &\equiv a_n^5 + 10a_n^3 + 5a_n \pmod{25} 
5b_{n+1} + 1 &\equiv (5b_n + 1)^5 + 10(5b_n + 1)^3 + 5(5b_n + 1) \pmod{25} 
5b_{n+1} + 1 &\equiv 1 + 10 + 5 \pmod{25} 
b_{n+1} &\equiv 3 \pmod{5} 
\end{align*}
Thus,  $b_n \equiv 3\pmod{5}$  for  $n\ge 3$ .

Finally, let  $b_n = 5c_n + 3$  for  $n\ge 3$ . Again by the Binomial Theorem,
\begin{align*}
a_{n+1} &\equiv a_n^5 + 10a_n^3 + 5a_n \pmod{125} 
5b_{n+1} + 1 &\equiv (5b_n + 1)^5 + 10(5b_n + 1)^3 + 5(5b_n + 1) \pmod{125} 
5b_{n+1} + 1 &\equiv 25b_n + 1 + 150b_n + 10 + 25b_n + 5 \pmod{125} 
5b_{n+1} &\equiv 200b_n + 15 \pmod{125} 
b_{n+1} &\equiv 40b_n + 3\pmod{25} 
5c_{n+1}+3 &\equiv 40(5c_n + 3) + 3\pmod{25} 
5c_{n+1} &\equiv 120 \equiv 20\pmod{25} 
c_{n+1} &\equiv 4\pmod{5}
\end{align*}
Thus,  $c_n\equiv 4\pmod{5}$  for  $n\ge 4$ . This means  $a_n \equiv 5(5\cdot 4 + 3) + 1 \equiv 116\pmod{125}$  for  $n\ge 4$ .

Now, routine CRT computation gives  $a_n\equiv \boxed{616}\pmod{1000}$  for  $n\ge 4$ .

