Let  $\omega$ ,  $\Omega$  be the incircle and circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  respectively. Let  $DE, BC$  touch  $\omega$  at  $K_1, K_2$  respectively. Note that  $K_1, K_2$  are antipodes in  $\omega$  since  $DE \parallel BC$ . Further, drop perpendiculars from  $A$  onto  $DE$  and  $BC$  at  $Y, X$  respectively. Note that  $A, X, Y$  are collinear. Also, since  $DEBC$  is cyclic, and a trapezoid, it is isosceles implying that  $\angle DAB = \angle CAE$  or that  $AB, AC$  are isogonal with respect to  $\triangle ADE$ .  We consider the  $\sqrt{bc}$ -inversion in triangle  $\triangle ADE$ , that is, we invert around  $A$  with radius  $\sqrt{AD \cdot AE}$  and then reflect about the angle bisector of  $\angle DAE$ , which is just  $AI$ . Under the mentioned transform,  $AB \to AC$  (the lines are isogonal) and  $\Omega \to DE$  (well known). This implies that the image of  $B = AB \cap \Omega$  maps to  $AC \cap DE$ , which we will call  $B_{*}$ . Similarily define  $C_{*}$ . Note that  $\triangle AC_{*}B_{*}$  is similar to  $\triangle ABC$  since  $DE \parallel BC$ . In particular  $B_{*}C_{*}/BC = AY/AX = 1-\dfrac{K_1K_2}{h_a} = 1-\dfrac{2r}{h_a}$ . Writing  $2r = 2\Delta/s$ , we find  $\dfrac{B_{*}C_{*}}{BC} = \dfrac{s-a}{s}$  (we have used  $ah_a = 2\Delta$ ) \newline
Now we make use of the inversion distance formula. Indeed, this yields that  $(\sqrt{AD \cdot AE})^2 = AB \cdot AC \cdot \dfrac{B_{*}C_{*}}{BC} = \dfrac{bc(s-a)}{s}$ . But now it is well known that the previous experession is simply  $AI^2$ . Thus this yields that \[AI^2 = AD \cdot AE\] as needed.</details>

