By symmetry, XZ bisects the angle  $\theta$  and hence  $\angle ZXW = \frac{\theta}{2}$ . Now the fun begins. Note that  $YZ^2 = XY^2 - XZ^2$ . Let  $XY=x$ , the side of one of the rhombuses. Then by trig,  $WX = x\cos{\theta}$ , and so  $XZ = \frac{x\cos{\theta}}{\sin{\frac{\theta}{2}}}$ , so we have that  $YZ=\sqrt{x^2 - \frac{x^2\cos^2{\theta}}{\sin^2{\frac{\theta}{2}}}}$ . This is the height of the first parallelepiped. For the height of the second one, simply just change the location of  $X$  to be on the  $180-\theta$  angle, repeating all the above calculation, giving  $YZ=\sqrt{x^2 - \frac{x^2\cos^2{\theta}}{\sin^2({90^\circ - \frac{\theta}{2}})}}$ .

