Now, note that  $\tan{\frac{\theta}{2}}$  is simply just the ratio of the smaller diagonal to the longer diagonal. With a bit of basic manipulation, and the fact that  $\theta$  is acute, we have  $\sin{\frac{\theta}{2}}=\sqrt{\frac{21}{52}}$  and  $\cos{\frac{\theta}{2}}=\sqrt{\frac{31}{52}}$ . Thus  $\cos{\theta}=\frac{5}{26}$ , using traditional double angle identities. Plugging this in, we have
