Let the triangle  $T$  have vertices  $A$ ,  $B$ ,  $C$ . Let  $P$  be the point inside  $T$  which is closest (in distance) to line  $BC$ . Then, it follows that there are no lattice points lying inside or on  $\triangle PBC$ . Consequently, by Pick's theorem,  $\triangle PBC$  has area  $1/2$ .
Now, consider the vectors  $\mathbf w = \vec B - \vec C$  and  $\mathbf v = \vec P - \vec C$ , and situate  $C$  at the origin.**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** Every lattice point can be written uniquely as  $n \mathbf w + k \mathbf v$  for integers  $n$  and  $k$ .
*Proof.* Indeed, it is obvious every pair  $(n, k)$  gives a lattice point and uniqueness follows from the fact that  $\mathbf w$  and  $\mathbf v$  are linearly independent.
Existence is the interesting part. This is really a statement about  $\operatorname{GL}_2({\mathbb Z})$  but one can give an elementary proof using areas again. Let  $Q$  be any other point. By Pick's theorem,  $2[QBC]$  is also an integer, say  $n$ . So  $Q$  should lie on the line through  $n \cdot \mathbf v$ , parallel to line  $BC$ . Since  $\mathbf w$  was a minimal length lattice vector along this direction, it follows that  $\vec Q - n \mathbf v$  is an integer multiple of  $\mathbf w$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Now, we return to the main problem. Again by Pick's theorem,  $[ABC] = 9/2$ . So in fact  $A = 9 \mathbf v + k \mathbf w$  for some integer  $k$ . We see that in order for  $ABC$  to contain  $P$  in its interior, we must have  $0 < k < 9$ . So there are only eight possibilities for  $k$  to check.

[asy]
size(14cm); defaultpen(fontsize(11pt)); pair O = (0,0); pair B = (-2,-1); pair P = (-1,0);
transform t = rotate(-26.57);
pair A(int i) { return 9*P-i*B; } for (int i=0; i<=9; ++i) { if (i > 0) draw( A(i-1)--A(i), blue, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins ); } fill(B--A(4)--O--cycle, palegreen); int M = 13; for (int i = -M; i <= M; ++i) { draw( (i,-M)--(i,M), lightgrey ); draw( (-M,i)--(M,i), lightgrey ); }
draw(O--A(0), orange+dashed); draw(B--A(9), orange+dashed); draw(B--O, red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); draw(O--P, red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins);
picture rotated = t*currentpicture; currentpicture.erase(); add(rotated);
dot(" $C$ ", t*O, dir(-90)); dot(" $B$ ", t*B, dir(-90)); dot(" $P$ ", t*P, dir(45));
dot(" $9 \mathbf v$ ", t*A(0), dir(90), blue); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + \mathbf w$ ", t*A(1), dir(-90), deepcyan); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 2 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(2), dir(90), blue); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 3 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(3), dir(-90), blue); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 4 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(4), dir(90), deepcyan); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 5 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(5), dir(-90), blue); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 6 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(6), dir(90), blue); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 7 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(7), dir(-90), deepcyan); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 8 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(8), dir(90), blue); dot(" $9 \mathbf v + 9 \mathbf w$ ", t*A(9), dir(-90), blue); label(" $\mathbf v$ ", t*P/2, dir(15), red); label(" $\mathbf w$ ", t*B/2, dir(-90), red); label(" $A$ ", t*A(4), dir(-45));
clip(box((-9,-2),(14,7)));
[/asy]
Next, note that:  
- If  $k \equiv 0 \pmod 3$  then line  $AC$  contains the lattice point  $3 \mathbf v + \frac k3 \mathbf w$ , contradiction.
- If  $k \equiv 2 \pmod 3$  then line  $BC$  contains the lattice point  $3 \mathbf v + \frac{k+1}{3} \mathbf w$ , contradiction. [/list] So there are only three cases left to examine:  $k=1$ ,  $k=4$ ,  $k=7$ . We handle them now: [list]
- If  $k=1$  then  $ABC$  contains the four collinear lattice points  $\mathbf v$ ,  $2\mathbf v$ ,  $3\mathbf v$ ,  $4\mathbf v$ . (Incidentally, this is collinear with  $C$ .)
- If  $k=4$  then  $ABC$  contains the four collinear lattice points  $\mathbf v$ ,  $3\mathbf v + \mathbf w$ ,  $5\mathbf v + 2 \mathbf w$ ,  $7 \mathbf v + 3\mathbf v$ . (Incidentally, this is collinear with  $A$ .)
- If  $k=7$  then  $ABC$  contains the four collinear lattice points  $\mathbf v$ ,  $2\mathbf v + \mathbf w$ ,  $3\mathbf v + 2 \mathbf w$ ,  $4 \mathbf v + 3\mathbf v$ . (Incidentally, this is collinear with  $B$ .)
 Thus in all cases, the four lattice points are collinear, as desired.**<span style="color:red">Remark:</span>** I find it great that the result is false if  $4$  is replaced by the *smaller* number  $3$ .

