Let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{KL}$ .**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** [Blue part of the figure] Lines  $\overline{AM}$  and  $\overline{AD}$  are isogonal to  $\angle A$ , and  $AM = AD \cdot \cos A$ .
*Proof.* First part follows from  $\overline{AD}$  being the  $A$ -symmedian. For the second part, the law of sines gives \begin{align*} \frac{AD}{LD} &= \frac{\sin \angle ALD}{\sin \angle DAL} = \frac{\sin(180^{\circ} - \angle K)}{\sin \angle KAM} = \frac{\sin \angle K}{\sin \angle KAM} = \frac{AM}{KM}  \implies AM &= AD \cdot \frac{KM}{LD} = AD \cdot \frac{LM}{LD} = AD \cos \angle KLD = AD \cos A. \end{align*}  $\blacksquare$ Let  $H$  denote the orthocenter.**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** [Red part of the figure] Lines  $\overline{AH}$  and  $\overline{AO}$  are isogonal to  $\angle A$ , and  $AH = AD \cdot \cos A$ .
*Proof.* Both parts are well-known.  $\blacksquare$ The two claims together imply  $H=M$  and we're done.**<span style="color:red">Remark:</span>** The fact that  $MBDC$  is a parallelogram is a known result and can be proven by more classical means such as angle chasing; see [Russia 2013 / 9.2](https://aops.com/community/c6h535000p3067553). This lemma is also the key result needed for [https://aops.com/community/p15680911](https://aops.com/community/p15680911){AIME II 2020 Problem 15}.

