In particular, the gardner just splits up the grid into 3x3 squares, and repeatedly goes around tending to each of the  $\left( \frac{2022}{3} \right)^2$  squares. After  $\left( \frac{2022}{3} \right)^2 \cdot n$  gardner-lumberjack turns, the gardner has touched each square  $n$  times.

For the sake of contradiction, assume that at this point, the lumberjack has managed to stop  $4 \cdot \left( \frac{2022}{3} \right)^2 + 1$  trees from growing. Then, he must have touched each of those trees at least  $n-(10^6 - 1)$  times. So, he has made a total of
