draw((x,0)--(x,9));
}
for(int y = 0; y <= 9; ++y) {
draw((0,y)--(9,y));
}
[/asy]

from which it's clear that every  $3 \times 3$  square contains exactly four colored cells. Then, whenever Alice waters a square, Bob chops in the gray cells. Thus, no tree can ever have positive height in the gray cells, which proves the bound.

Now, we show Alice can ensure the eventual existence of  $5 \cdot 674^2$  majestic trees. First, she waters the first  $3 \times 3$  square  $N_1$  times, where  $N_1$  is *extremely* large, guaranteeing at least  $5$  majestic trees in this square. Then, she moves on, watering the second  $3 \times 3$  square  $N_2 < N_1$  times, where  $N_2$  is chosen such that Bob cannot undo her progress in the first square in  $N_2$  turns. Alice repeats this process until the last  $3 \times 3$  square has been watered  $N_{674^2}$  times. This does the trick, since she can always choose  $(N_i)$  sufficiently large.  $\square$

