draw(A--T, fuchsia);
draw(X--T, fuchsia);
draw(Y--T, dashed+fuchsia);
draw(A--X, fuchsia);
draw(A--Y, fuchsia);
draw(P--D, orange);
draw(P--E, orange);
draw(D--A, lightcyan);
draw(D--X, lightcyan);
draw(E--A, springgreen);
draw(E--Y, springgreen);
draw(circumcircle(A,B,D), red);
draw(circumcircle(A,E,C), red);
dot(" $A$ ", A, dir(A));
dot(" $B$ ", B, dir(B));
dot(" $C$ ", C, dir(C));
dot(" $F$ ", F, dir(320));
dot(" $P$ ", P, dir(30));
dot(" $D$ ", D, dir(D));
dot(" $E$ ", E, dir(E));
dot(" $T$ ", T, dir(T));
dot(" $X$ ", X, dir(X));
dot(" $Y$ ", Y, dir(Y));
dot(" $Q$ ", Q, dir(235));
[/asy]  **<span style="color:red">Claim:**</span>  $F$  is on the radical axis of  $\odot(ABD)$  and  $\odot(AEC)$ .  $BC$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AT}$ . 

*Proof:* From  $\triangle PED \sim \triangle ABC$ , we have \begin{align*} \frac{FE}{FB}              & = \frac{FD}{FC}          \iff \overline{FE} \cdot \overline{FC} & = \overline{FD} \cdot \overline{FB}  \iff \mathrm{Pow}(F, \odot(AEC))   & = \mathrm{Pow}(F, \odot(ABD)) \end{align*} which shows the first part of the claim. For the second part, since  $BF \perp AT$ , it is sufficient to show  $\measuredangle ABF = \measuredangle FBT$ . Now 
\[\measuredangle FBT = \measuredangle DAX = \measuredangle AXD = \measuredangle ABF\] 
and the claim holds true.  $\square$ -----------------------------------
We now show that  $T$  lies on  $CY$  as well. This fact follows from the following angle chase keeping in mind that  $C$  is on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AT}$  \begin{align*} \measuredangle TCY &= \measuredangle TCA + \measuredangle ACY  &= 2\measuredangle ECA + \measuredangle AEY  &= 2\measuredangle EYA + \measuredangle AEY = 0^\circ \end{align*} which proves the collinearity. To conclude, by Power of a Point we have 
\[\overline{TB} \cdot \overline{TX} = \overline{TQ} \cdot \overline{TA} = \overline{TC}\cdot \overline{TY}\] 
which gives  $BXCY$  concylic as requested.  $\blacksquare$

