The answer is  $k+4$ . The construction is the integers from  $-\left\lfloor \frac k2 \right\rfloor -2$  to  $\left\lfloor \frac k2 \right\rfloor +2$  inclusive, except we remove  $0$  for even  $k$ . I honestly don't care enough to prove this works just do stuff.

Let  $a_1 < a_2 < \dots < a_n$ . The bound is obtained by noting that 
\begin{align*}
a_1 &\geq a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{k+1}
a_n &\leq a_{n-k} + a_{n-k+1} + \dots + a_{n-1}
\end{align*}
Then 
\[
a_3 + \dots + a_{k+1} < 0 < a_{n-k} + a_{n-k+1} + \dots + a_{n-1},
\]
and then  $n-k >3$  or  $n\geq k+4$ .

Let  $a_1<a_2<\dots<a_n$  be members of the set with described properties. Note that 
\begin{align*}
a_1 &\ge a_2+a_3+\dots+a_{k+1}
a_n &\le a_{n-k}+a_{n-k+1}+\dots+a_{n-1}
\end{align*}
The first gives
\[a_3+\dots+a_{k+1}<0\]
While the second gives 
\[a_{n-k}+\dots+a_{n-1}>0\]
so  $n-k>3$  which implies  $n\ge k+4$  which is the answer. The construction is omitted.

We claim the answer is  $n=k+4$ .

If  $k=2\ell$  is even, we take \[-F_{\ell+3},\dots,-F_3,-F_2,F_2,F_3,\dots,F_{\ell+3}\] and if  $k=2\ell+1$  is odd, we take \[-F_{\ell+3},\dots,-F_3,-F_2,0,F_2,F_3,\dots,F_{\ell+3}\] In either case, to get the sum  $F_t$ , start with  $F_{t+1}+(-F_{t-1})$  if  $t<\ell+3$  (noting that  $F_1=F_2$  if  $t=2$ ) and  $F_{t-1}+F_{t-2}$  if  $t=\ell+3$ , and add  $\ell-1$  other pairs of additive inverse summands, and if  $k$  is odd then add the summand  $0$ . Negative elements can similarly be obtained. If  $k$  is odd, then to get the sum  $0$ , start with  $F_4+(-F_3)+(-F_2)$ , and add  $\ell-1$  other pairs of additive inverse summands. This shows that  $n=k+4$  is obtainable.

Now suppose FTSOC  $n<k+4$ . Suppose we have elements  $a_1<a_2<\dots<a_n$ . If  $n=k+1$ , then
\begin{align*}
&\begin{cases} a_n=a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{n-1} a_1=a_2+a_3+\dots+a_n\end{cases}
\implies&a_n=a_1
\end{align*}
If  $n=k+2$ , then
\begin{align*}
&\begin{cases} a_n\leq a_2+a_3+\dots+a_{n-1} a_1\geq a_2+a_3+\dots+a_{n-1}\end{cases}
\implies& a_n\leq a_1
\end{align*}
If  $n=k+3$ , then
\begin{align*}
&\begin{cases} a_n\leq a_3+a_4+\dots+a_{n-1} a_1\geq a_2+a_3+\dots+a_{n-2}\end{cases}
\implies& 2a_n+a_1+a_2\leq 2a_1+a_{n-1}+a_n
\implies& a_n+a_2\leq a_{n-1}+a_1
\end{align*}
In any case, we get a contradiction, as desired.

