Interesting problem, although I managed to convince myself that all  $\{c\}\ge 0.5$  worked at some point. 

We claim that the answer is all positive reals  $c$  for which  $\{c\}=0.5$  (fractional part). We first observe that we require  $c\ge 0$  for obvious reasons since if  $c<0$  ,  $f(x)-cx > |c|x > 2023$  for all sufficiently large positive integers  $x$ . We now look at our key observation.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :**For all positive integers  $x$  and  $n$ , 
    \[f(x+2^{n+1}) \equiv f(x) \pmod{2^n}\]</span>

*Proof :*We attack this via induction. Note that since  $f(x+2) \not \equiv f(x) \pmod{2}$  and  $f(x+4) \not \equiv f(x+2) \pmod{2}$  and there are only two possible residue classes  $\pmod{2}$ , both  $f(x)$  and  $f(x+4)$  must be of the same residue class, showing the base case.

    For the inductive step, assume  $f(x+2^r) \equiv f(x) \pmod{2^{r-1}}$  and  $f(x+2^{r+1}) \equiv f(x+2^r) \pmod{2^{r-1}}$ . However, since  $f(x+2^r)\not \equiv f(x) \pmod{2^r}$  they must be of the two different possible residue classes  $\pmod{2^r}$ . Similarly  $f(x+2^r)$  and  $f(x+2^{r+1})$  are of the two different possible residue classes  $\pmod{2^r}$  implying that  $f(x) \equiv f(x+2^{r+1}) \pmod{2^r}$ . Hence the inductive step is complete, proving the claim.

We now show that all  $\{c\}\ne 0.5$  do not work. For this, note that the second bullet point translates to the condition that, 
\[cx-2023<f(x)<cx+2023\]
for all positive integers  $x$ . Now if  $\{c\}<0.5$  we note that, 
\begin{align*}
    f(x+2^{n+1}) & < cx + c\cdot 2^{n+1} + 2023
    &= cx + \lfloor c\rfloor\cdot 2^{n+1} + \{c\}\cdot 2^{n+1} + 2023
    & < cx + \lfloor c\rfloor\cdot 2^{n+1} + 2^n - 2023
    & < f(x) + 2^n\left(2\lfloor c\rfloor +1\right)
\end{align*}
for all sufficiently large positive integers  $n$ . Further, 
\begin{align*}
    f(x+2^{n+1}) & > cx+ c \cdot 2^{n+1}-2023
    & > cx + \lfloor c\rfloor \cdot 2^{n+1}+2023
    & >f(x)+ 2^n \left(2\lfloor c\rfloor\right)
\end{align*}
for all sufficiently large  $n$ . But since  $f(x+2^{n+1}) \equiv f(x) \pmod{2^{n}}$  this is a very clear contradiction, implying that  $\{c\}\ge 0.5$ . Further more if  $\{c\}>0.5$  we note that, 
\begin{align*}
    f(x+2^{n+1}) &< cx + c\cdot 2^{n+1} + 2023
    &= cx + \lfloor c\rfloor\cdot 2^{n+1} + \{c\}\cdot 2^{n+1} + 2023 
    & < cx + \lfloor c\rfloor\cdot 2^{n+1} + 2^{n+1} - 2023 
    & < f(x) + 2^n \left(2\lfloor c\rfloor + 2 \right)
\end{align*}
for all sufficiently large positive integers  $n$ . And finally, 
\begin{align*}
    f(x+2^{n+1}) & > cx+ c \cdot 2^{n+1}-2023
    &= cx + \lfloor c\rfloor\cdot 2^{n+1} + \{c\}\cdot 2^{n+1} - 2023 
    & > cx + \lfloor c\rfloor \cdot 2^{n+1}+2^n + 2023 
    & > f(x) + 2^n \left(2\lfloor c\rfloor +1\right)
\end{align*}
for all sufficiently large  $n$ . But since  $f(x+2^{n+1}) \equiv f(x) \pmod{2^{n}}$  this is a very clear contradiction, implying that  $\{c\}\le 0.5$ . The contradictory inequalities indeed allow us to conclude that  $\{c\}=0.5$ . 

We now provide a construction for all  $c$  that do work. Simply consider the function  $f(x) = \lceil cx \rceil$ . Clearly this function satisfies the domain and codomain restrictions and the second bullet point. Let  $c = n+\frac{1}{2}$  for som non-negative integer  $n$ . Then, 
\begin{align*}
    f(x+a)-f(x)&= \lceil cx+ca \rceil - \lceil cx \rceil 
    & = \left \lceil nx + na + \frac{x+a}{2} \right \rceil - \left \lceil nx + \frac{x}{2} \right \rceil 
    &= na + \left \lceil \frac{x+a}{2} \right \rceil - \left \lceil \frac{x}{2} \right \rceil 
\end{align*}
However since  $a \ge 2$  , 
\[a\ge \frac{a+2}{2}=\frac{x+a}{2}+1-\frac{x}{2}>\left \lceil \frac{x+a}{2} \right \rceil - \left \lceil \frac{x}{2} \right \rceil  > \frac{x+a}{2} - \frac{x}{2}-1 = \frac{a}{2}-1 \ge 0\]
which implies that  $f(x+a)-f(x)$  is wedged in between two consecutive positive integral multiples of  $a$  which confirms the first bullet point.

