Let  $\beta = \angle HBC$  and  $\gamma = \angle HCB$ Let  $\phi$  be the  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion of triangle  $BHC$  (with the center  $H$ .) Let  $P' = \phi(P)$ . Thus,  $\angle CBP' = 2\beta$  and  $\angle BCP' = 2 \gamma .$ [rule]**<span style="color:#00f">Claim 1:</span>**  $A$  is the Miquel point of quadrilateral  $BXYC$ .
<span style="color:#f00">Proof:</span> Let  $Q$  be the intersection of  $BX$  and  $CY$ . Note that  $\triangle HBX \equiv \triangle HPB \overset{+}{\sim} \triangle HCP'$ , so  $\angle XBC = \beta +\angle HP'C.$  Similarly,  $\angle YCB = \gamma + \angle HP'B$ . By <details><summary>enjoyable angle-chasing</summary>\begin{align*}
\angle BQC &= 180^\circ - \angle XBC - \angle YCB 
&= 180^\circ - (\beta + \gamma) - (\angle HP'C + \angle HP'B) 
&= 180^\circ - (\beta + \gamma) - (180^\circ - \angle P'BC - \angle P'CB) 
&= 180^\circ - (\beta + \gamma) - (180^\circ - 2\beta - 2\gamma) 
&= \beta + \gamma 
&= \angle BAC 
\end{align*}</details>,  $B,C,Q,A$  are concyclic. 

It remains to prove that  $BX / BA = CY / CA$  by<details><summary>the well-known Miquel lemma</summary>Since  $B,C,Q,A$ , we have  $\angle XBA = YCA$ . With what we will prove,  $\triangle XBA \sim \triangle YCA$ , so  $\angle QXA = \angle QYA$  and  $Q,A,X,Y$  are concylic.</details>which can be done by<details><summary>not so funny side-chasing</summary>\begin{align*}\frac{BX}{CY} &= \frac{BP}{ CP} = \frac{P'C \cdot AP}{HC} \Big / \frac{P'B \cdot AP}{BH} &= \frac{P'C}{P'B} \cdot \frac{BH}{CH} = \frac{\sin 2\beta}{\sin 2\gamma} \cdot \frac{\sin \gamma}{\sin \beta} &= \frac{\cos \beta}{\cos \gamma} = \frac{BA}{CA}\end{align*}.</details>.  $\quad \square$ [rule]**<span style="color:#00f">Claim 2:</span>**  $XY \perp HP'$ <span style="color:#f00">Proof:</span> Note that  $HX = HP = HY$ , so  $XY$  is perpendicular to internal bisector of  $\angle XHY$  which is  $HP'$ .[rule]
Let  $O$  be the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$  and  $A' = \phi(A)$ . Not hard to see that  $A',P'$  are symmetric wrt  $BC$ , so  $A'H' \perp BC$ .

We need to prove that  $\angle BAS = CAP$  which is equivalent to  $\angle HAP = \angle OAS$ . Since  $\angle HAP = \angle HP'A'$  and, by spiral similarity with a Miquel point,  $\angle(XY,BC) = \angle OAS$ , it suffices to show that  $\angle HP'A' = \angle(XY,BC)$  which is  $XY \perp HP'$ , the claim we already proved.
[asy]
import geometry;
import olympiad;
import graph;
size(300);

pair A,B,C,H,P,PP,X,Y,S,Q;

B = dir(145);
C = dir(35);
PP = dir(-110);
pair I= incenter(B,C,PP);
pair HH = foot(I,B,C);
H = 2 * HH - I;
P = (B-H) * (C-H) / (PP-H) + H;
X = 2*foot(P,B,H) - P;
Y = 2*foot(P,C,H) - P;
A = orthocenter(B,C,H);
S = circumcenter(A,X,Y);
Q = extension(B,X,C,Y);

fill(A--B--C--cycle, 0.1*cyan + 0.9*white);
fill(B--H--PP--cycle,0.3*green + 0.7*white);
fill(C--H--P--cycle,0.3*green + 0.7*white);
fill(C--H--Y--cycle,0.3*green + 0.7*white);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(circumcircle(A,B,C));
draw(circumcircle(A,X,Y));
draw(B--Q--C);
draw(X--H--Y);
draw(B--H--C);
draw(B--PP--C);
draw(PP--S,purple);
draw(P--B);
draw(P--C);
draw(P--H);

dot(" $B$ ",B,dir(210));
dot(" $C$ ",C,dir(-30));
dot(" $P'$ ",PP,dir(PP));
dot(" $H$ ",H,2*dir(110));
dot(" $P$ ",P,1.5*dir(-90));
dot(" $A$ ",A,dir(A));
dot(" $X$ ",X,dir(200));
dot(" $Y$ ",Y,2.5*dir(10));
dot(" $Q$ ",Q,dir(Q));
dot(" $S$ ",S,dir(S));
dot(" $A'$ ",2*foot(PP,B,C) - PP,dir(S));
[/asy]

