Finally got around to doing this one.  I realized while scrolling through the thread that my original solution missed a case; hopefully, this proof is now correct.  It finishes slightly differently than others, I think.

As everyone else does, rewrite the system of equations in the form
\begin{align*}
a^n - b^n &= p(c-b), 
b^n - c^n &= p(a-c),
c^n - a^n &= p(b-a).
\end{align*}
First suppose  $n$  is odd.  Without loss of generality, let  $b$  be the *middle* value of the set  $\{a,b,c\}$ .  Because  $n$  is odd,  $x^n - y^n$  and  $x-y$  have the same sign for any real numbers  $x$  and  $y$ .  It follows that  $a^n - b^n$  and  $c - b$  have opposite signs, contradicting the first equation unless both integers are zero.  That is,  $a^n = b^n$  and  $c = b$ , so  $a = b = c$ .

The more difficult case occurs when  $n$  is even.  Suppose, by way of contradiction, that none of  $a$ ,  $b$ , or  $c$  are equal.  Multiplying all three equations yields
\[
(a^n - b^n)(b^n - c^n)(c^n - a^n) = p^3(c-b)(a-c)(b-a),
\]
or
\[
\frac{a^n - b^n}{a - b}\cdot \frac{b^n - c^n}{b - c} \cdot \frac{c^n - a^n}{c - a} = p^3.
\]
All three factors on the left hand side are integers, and these three integers multiply to  $p^3$ .  It follows that the absolute values of the factors on the left are  $\{p,p,p\}$  or  $\{1,p,p^2\}$ .  In particular, some factor is equal to either  $p$  or  $-p$ .

There are two remaining cases to check.  First suppose some factor (without loss  $\tfrac{a^n - b^n}{a-b}$ ) is equal to  $p$ .  Then
\[
p(a-b) = a^n - b^n = p(c-b),
\]
so  $a - b = c - b$  and  $a = c$ .  This contradicts our original assumption that no two variables are equal to each other.

Now suppose some factor (again without loss  $\tfrac{a^n - b^n}{a - b}$ ) is equal to  $-p$ .  Then
\begin{align*}
p(b-a) &= a^n - b^n = p(c-b)\quad\text{and}
c^n - a^n &= p(b - a) = a^n - b^n
\end{align*}
so  $2b = a + c$  and  $2b^n = a^n + c^n$ .  Because  $n$  is even,  $a^n + c^n > 0$ , so  $b \neq 0$ .  This means we may divide the first equation by  $b\neq 0$  and the second equation by  $b^n \neq 0$ .  Letting  $x := \tfrac{a}{b}$ ,  $y := \tfrac{c}{b}$  thus yields the system of equations 
\[
x + y = 2 = x^n + y^n.
\]
However, because  $n$  is even, the set  $\{(x,y): x^n + y^n = 2\}$  is a convex set, symmetric about the origin and tangent to the line  $x + y = 2$  at  $(x,y) = (1,1)$ .  Thus  $\tfrac ab = \tfrac cb = 1$  and  $a = b = c$ .
[asy]
size(170);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.6)+fontsize(10));
int n = 4;
real f(real x)
{
return (2 - x^4)^(1/4);
}
real g(real x)
{
return -(2 - x^4)^(1/4);
}
draw(graph(f,-2^(1/4),2^(1/4),n=500));
draw(graph(g,-2^(1/4),2^(1/4),n=500));
label(" $x^n+y^n=2$ ",(-1.3,1.3));
draw((-0.1,2.1)--(2.1,-0.1),Arrows(size=4));
label(" $x+y=2$ ",(1.4,1.3));
[/asy]
<span style="color:#00f">EDIT 3:45 PM: It occurs to me, after the fact, that a more standard end is to use the inequality  $\color{blue}(x+y)^n \geq 2^{n-1}(x^n + y^n)$ .  This follows by Jensen on the function  $\color{blue}t\mapsto t^n$ , which is everywhere convex because  $\color{blue} n$  is even.</span>

